Key bugs: parse_u64 sets out=0 before validation → contract violation (out unchanged on failure). Also overflow: rejects values above UINT64_MAX? It wraps silently, no overflow check → accepts values that wrap (e.g. "18446744073789551617" wraps to 1) → contract violation. Also empty string: returns true with out=0 → accepts empty, contract violation. Also parse_u64 marked noexcept but fine.

Registry: get returns string_view into vector while holding lock only during call; readers may retain returned view after get returns while writers keep calling add → push_back may reallocate/move, invalidating the view → dangling string_view. Also get(i) no bounds check → UB for out-of-range i. Also get returns view to element that add may erase? No erase. Also names[i] with i >= size → UB.

Also add push_back with move: fine. But vector reallocation invalidates views. Minimal example: get(0) view retained, then add enough strings to force reallocation → view dangles. Also even without reallocation, push_back of a new element doesn't invalidate existing views (only reallocation or move does). Actually push_back may move elements? Move occurs when capacity exceeded → reallocation. So fix: store shared_ptr or return std::string by value, or use deque/linked list of stable addresses, or copy-on-write... Simplest: return std::string (copy) or keep views valid by storing elements in stable storage (e.g. std::deque<std::string> or vector of unique_ptr/shared_ptr so pointers remain stable).

Also get doesn't bounds-check; contract violation with i >= names.size().

Also mutex held during get returning view: fine but readers retaining view after get returns while writers add → dangling. Fix: return std::string_view but ensure stable addresses: store std::vector<std::unique_ptr<std::string>> or deque; or return std::string by value.

Also parse_u64 overflow: need check out > (UINT64_MAX - digit)/10 → return false. Also empty: for empty loop doesn't run → returns true, violating "nonempty" contract. Also leading zeros allowed: fine. Also out=0 pre-assignment violates unchanged-on-failure: must only assign at end. Fix: accumulate in local, assign out only on success.

Also noexcept fine since no throwing.

Also "rejects values above UINT64_MAX": overflow check needed.

Also non-ASCII: c<0? char may be negative for bytes >127? c is char, signed; c<'0' catches negative fine. ASCII check ok.

Count findings: parse_u64: (1) out=0 before validation → out changed on failure; (2) empty accepted; (3) overflow wraps, no rejection. Registry: (4) get returns view that dangles after add reallocation; (5) no bounds check → UB. Also (6) maybe mutex not protecting retained view lifetime — same as 4. Also style: lock_guard fine.

Also get returns string_view while holding lock: view refers to element; after unlock, writers may add → reallocation invalidates. Fix: return std::string (copy) or keep views valid by storing elements in stable storage (e.g. std::deque<std::string> or vector of unique_ptr/shared_ptr so pointers remain stable).

Minimal examples: parse_u64("12x", out) leaves out=0 though caller expected unchanged; parse_u64("") returns true; parse_u64("18446744073789551617") returns true wrapping to 1 (above max). Registry: r.get(0) view kept, then add 20 strings forcing reallocation → dangling; r.get(5) with size 2 → UB.

Corrected parse_u64 in one cpp block:

```cpp
bool parse_u64(std::string_view s, std::uint64_t& out) noexcept {
    std::uint64_t v = 0;
    if (s.empty()) return false;
    for (char c : s) {
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') return false;
        std::uint64_t d = c - '0';
        if (v > (UINT64_MAX - d) / 10) return false;
        v = v * 10 + d;
    }
    out = v;
    return true;
}
```

Need include <limits>? Use std::numeric_limits<std::uint64_t>::max(). Fine.

Word budget ~650. Keep concise.
